The main change replaces the `memcmp` against the invalid sentinel with a fixed-width unsigned-integer comparison.

- **Why it is faster:** this is a single register compare. It avoids relying on the compiler to inline `memcmp` and drops the extra load through a byte pointer.
- **Why behavior is unchanged:** the comparison is still byte-exact.
  - For floating-point `TFrom`, comparing as `float`/`double` would treat the NaN invalid pattern differently, so bits are compared instead.
  - For other sizes, the code falls back to the original `memcmp`.
- **Other changes:** the invalid-value return now uses `memcpy` rather than a `reinterpret_cast` read. This gives the same value without aliasing or alignment issues. `GetType()` is now only evaluated after the value is read. This assumes `GetType()` is a plain getter with no side effects.

```cpp
template <typename TTo, typename TFrom>
TTo FieldBase::ConvertBaseType(const FIT_UINT8 fieldArrayIndex, const FIT_UINT8 toBaseType) const
{
    const TFrom value = GetValue<TFrom>(fieldArrayIndex);
    FIT_CONST_UINT8_PTR invalid = baseTypeInvalids[GetType() & FIT_BASE_TYPE_NUM_MASK];

    bool isInvalid;
    if constexpr (sizeof(TFrom) == 1 || sizeof(TFrom) == 2 || sizeof(TFrom) == 4 || sizeof(TFrom) == 8)
    {
        using Bits = std::conditional_t<sizeof(TFrom) == 1, std::uint8_t,
                     std::conditional_t<sizeof(TFrom) == 2, std::uint16_t,
                     std::conditional_t<sizeof(TFrom) == 4, std::uint32_t, std::uint64_t>>>;
        Bits a;
        Bits b;
        std::memcpy(&a, &value, sizeof(Bits));
        std::memcpy(&b, invalid, sizeof(Bits));
        isInvalid = (a == b);
    }
    else
    {
        isInvalid = (std::memcmp(&value, invalid, sizeof(TFrom)) == 0);
    }

    if (isInvalid)
    {
        TTo result;
        std::memcpy(&result, baseTypeInvalids[toBaseType & FIT_BASE_TYPE_NUM_MASK], sizeof(TTo));
        return result;
    }

    return static_cast<TTo>(value);
}
```

```includes
#include <cstring>
#include <cstdint>
#include <type_traits>
```